artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a relativistic collision of two heavy nuclei at high energy. At the moment just after the collision, the system can be described by QCD partonic degrees of freedom (quarks and gluons) that are highly anisotropic in momentum space and far from thermal equilibrium. Subsequent macroscopic observables (such as elliptic flow) are well reproduced by relativistic hydrodynamic models that take as their initial condition a locally thermalized quark–gluon plasma at proper time of order τ₀ ≲ 1 fm/c after the collision.

Standard parton-cascade descriptions, using elastic 2→2 perturbative QCD cross sections of order a few millibarns, yield equilibration times that are substantially longer than this τ₀. Moreover, momentum-space isotropization (e.g. driven by collective plasma instabilities or other mechanisms) is a necessary but not sufficient condition for the system to reach a local thermal equilibrium state with a well-defined temperature, equation of state, and approximately equilibrium phase-space distributions.

Provide a quantitative, controlled dynamical mechanism, derived from QCD or an effective theory of QCD, by which the initially produced out-of-equilibrium partonic system evolves into a locally thermalized quark–gluon plasma on a proper-time scale τ₀ ≲ 1 fm/c, consistent with the initial conditions required by successful hydrodynamic descriptions of heavy-ion collision data. The mechanism should:
(i) start from initial conditions appropriate to the immediate post-collision state of two colliding nuclei at collider energies (e.g. a color-glass-condensate-like configuration);
(ii) account for genuine thermalization, not merely isotropization, including approach of the longitudinal and transverse pressures to their equilibrium relation and the population of partonic phase space toward thermal distributions;
(iii) give a thermalization time consistent with τ₀ ≲ 1 fm/c using physically reasonable QCD couplings/cross sections.
